---
import ConceptLayout from '../../layouts/ConceptLayout.astro'
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro'
---
<ConceptLayout
	slug="mcp"
	source={[
		'apps/web/src/api/routes/mcp.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/routes/oauth.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/mcp/',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/agents/tools/surfaces.ts',
		'apps/web/src/ui/pages/settings/Mcp.tsx',
		'apps/web/tests/api/security/mcp-visibility.test.ts',
		'.claude/rules/tools.md',
		'.claude/skills/rf-mcp/',
	]}
>
	<h2>An AI client, acting as one person</h2>
	<p>
		From kit 0.41.0 the app is an MCP server. Claude (claude.ai, Claude Desktop and Claude Code) and
		ChatGPT connect to it at <code>/mcp</code>, and each connection acts as the one person who signed
		in, in one organisation, with that person's role and permissions and nothing more. A
		knowledge-base document shared with a group stays invisible to someone outside the group, exactly
		as it is in the browser. Support users cannot connect at all, and a client never carries a
		platform admin's reach, even when the person who connected it has it.
	</p>
	<p>
		Nothing about the person is cached on the connection. Every call reads their membership, role,
		groups and block status again, so a role change applies to the next call and a removed member's
		connections are revoked in the same transaction as the removal. The kit's own tools
		are the knowledge tools chat already uses: search, list and read documents, and look up facts.
		A test calls each one as an outsider and proves a group-only document, its facts and its
		entities do not exist for them, and that every answer matches what chat would give the same person.
	</p>

	<h2>Off until the deployment ships it and an organisation turns it on</h2>
	<p>
		The server is behind the environment-gated <code>mcp</code> <a href="/concepts/feature-flags/">feature
		flag</a>. Until a deployment lists <code>mcp</code> in <code>FEATURES_ENABLED</code>, every MCP and
		OAuth path answers 404. Once it ships, each organisation's owners and admins still have to turn it
		on, at Settings → MCP, where the rest of the controls live:
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>The switch</td><td>off by default; turning it off suspends every connection without revoking it</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Server URL</td><td>the organisation's own <code>/mcp/t/&lt;slug&gt;</code> under multi-tenancy, <code>/mcp</code> under single, with setup snippets for Claude and ChatGPT</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Who can connect</td><td>everyone, admins only, or chosen groups</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Exposure</td><td>read-only tools (the default), all tools after a confirmation, or a ticked selection</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Tools</td><td>grouped by provider, each with a read-only or writes badge and its 30-day call count; a tool that cannot reach a client is greyed with the reason</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Connected clients</td><td>every live connection: the person, the client, read or read-and-write, last used, with Revoke and Revoke all</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		Each person also sees their own connections, in every organisation, under Profile → Connected
		apps, and can revoke any of them there.
	</p>
	<Terminal
		title="zsh — connecting Claude Code"
		lines={[
			{ cmd: 'curl -si -X POST https://app.example.com/mcp | head -1' },
			{ out: 'HTTP/2 401' },
			{ cmd: 'claude mcp add --transport http acme-tools https://app.example.com/mcp/t/acme' },
		]}
		expect="Then /mcp inside Claude Code opens the app's own sign-in in the browser."
	/>

	<h2>OAuth 2.1, with the app as its own authorization server</h2>
	<p>
		A client never holds an API key, a session cookie or a CLI key: <code>/mcp</code> refuses all
		three. It holds an OAuth access token, issued by the app itself from tables in Postgres. The
		token lasts an hour, is stored only as a hash, belongs to a grant for one person, one
		organisation and one client, and is accepted only on the exact URL the grant was made for.
	</p>
	<p>
		Clients are identified by a <strong>client metadata document</strong> (CIMD): the client id is
		an https URL, and the app fetches the JSON document behind it to learn the client's name and
		redirect addresses. That fetch only goes to a public host on the default port, follows no
		redirect and reads at most 5 KB. There is no dynamic registration and no list of pre-registered
		clients. PKCE with S256 is mandatory, an authorization code is single-use and lives sixty
		seconds, and a refresh token rotates on every use.
	</p>
	<p>
		Sign-in is the app's own login page, so every sign-in method, the single-sign-on policy, the
		sign-up mode and blocking apply unchanged. The consent page then shows the client's name beside
		the host its id was verified on, the organisations where the person may connect, the scopes and
		the tools. There are two scopes: <code>mcp:read</code> and <code>mcp:write</code>. A tool that
		writes needs <code>mcp:write</code> as well as the person's own permission; a read-only
		connection that calls one is refused with 403 <code>insufficient_scope</code>, and the client
		asks the person to consent again.
	</p>

	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why the app's own login.</strong> An MCP connection is one more way for a person to
			act in an organisation, so it should be admitted by the same rules as every other way. Reusing
			the login page means an organisation that requires single sign-on, a deployment that is
			invite-only and a blocked account all behave on the OAuth path exactly as they do in the
			browser, with no second copy of those rules to keep in step.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Limits and the call log</h2>
	<p>
		Each request carries one call: a batch of JSON-RPC messages is refused, and no request is held
		open. Calls are capped per minute per connection (60), per person across all their connections
		in the organisation (120) and per organisation (600), checked in that order; each cap is a
		setting. A client over a cap gets a tool error telling it when to retry, not a 429. A model
		called by a tool is billed and capped as it is from chat, under the organisation's own limits.
	</p>
	<p>
		Every result opens with a line telling the client's model that it is data, not instructions,
		and is cut off at 60,000 characters. Each call is logged with who made it, through which
		connection, which tool, the outcome and how long it took, never its arguments or its result.
		The log is kept for 90 days, and each call is also a span in the trace.
	</p>

	<h2>One tool list for chat, agents and MCP</h2>
	<p>
		There is no separate MCP API to build. A tool is declared once and serves three surfaces: the
		chat panel, agent runs and the MCP server. <code>surfaces</code> keeps a tool off any of them
		(absent means all three), an optional <code>mcp</code> block sets the title and hints a client
		shows, and <code>toolsFor(surface, ctx)</code> is the one filter every surface calls. A tool
		marked as having side effects needs <code>mcp:write</code> and is hidden under the default
		read-only exposure. A tool with no handler, or one that needs a person's approval, is never
		offered over MCP.
	</p>
	<p>
		So a <a href="/concepts/plugins/">plugin's</a> tools reach MCP clients with no extra wiring: the
		plugin lists them in <code>tools</code>, with no route and no authentication of its own, and they
		appear under the plugin's name in Settings → MCP. The <a href="/concepts/analytics/">analytics
		plugin</a> offers its cube queries this way, to MCP clients and agent runs but not to chat.
	</p>

	<h2>Adding a tool with a coding agent</h2>
	<p>
		The kit ships an <code>/rf-mcp</code> skill that turns the server on for a deployment, adds a tool
		to the app or a plugin, connects Claude or ChatGPT to a local instance through a tunnel, and maps
		every 401, 403 and missing tool to its cause. When the agent opens a tool file, a rules file loads
		with the checklist for a safe tool: the tenant and the person come from the tool's context, never
		from its arguments; group-visible rows are read through the same visibility helpers the core tools
		use; every tool that writes, sends or charges says so; and no description names a brand.
	</p>
	<p>
		The test helper <code>callMcpTool</code> connects as a given member and calls a tool over the
		real <code>/mcp</code>, tokens and exposure included. Every tool owes three cases: a member of a
		second organisation sees none of the first's rows, a member outside a group does not see that
		group's rows, and a write tool is unlisted under read-only exposure and refused to a read-only token.
	</p>

	<h2>Under your name, not the kit's</h2>
	<p>
		Everything a client and its user see carries the deployment's own <code>APP_NAME</code> and the
		organisation's name: the server's name, its instructions, the consent page and every error. A
		test sets the app name to "Acme Tools" and fails if "Rocketflare" appears in any response, and
		<code>pnpm plugin check</code> warns on it in a plugin tool's title or description.
	</p>

	<div class="note">
		<p>
			What it does not do yet: only clients that publish a client metadata document can connect,
			which today means Claude and ChatGPT (Cursor only supports dynamic registration, and VS Code is
			untested). ChatGPT cannot reach a local <code>wrangler dev</code>, so test it on staging. The
			server offers tools only, with no resources or prompts, and renaming an organisation's slug
			breaks connections made on its URL until the client reconnects.
		</p>
	</div>
</ConceptLayout>
